3:PROVISIONAL. This is a design sketch, revision 8, written after eight Codex and agy review rounds. It folds in the Codex/agy/Kimi pipeline review and the owner's answers (2026-11-06). Build step 1 (BIR as the shared backend contract) is implemented: `Concrete/BIR.lean` and `Concrete/Backend/`, gate `scripts/checks/compiler/slice0_backends.sh`. Slice 0 is the first compiler slice. It is built before the owner freezes Core (A2c) and makes no normative claim before that freeze ([decision](../docs/decisions.md#decided-project-proof-infrastructure)). Branch `wip/slice0` depends on `wip/spec-root`, which puts `ConcreteSpec` on the new root, and is rebased onto main (under a new branch name) once that lands.
554:   - a **totality ban**: no `partial`, `unsafe`, `@[implemented_by]`, `@[extern]` or `opaque` in `Concrete/` outside `Premises.lean`;
582:8. **Reproducibility** (implemented, `scripts/checks/compiler/slice0_repro.sh`): two independent builds give byte-identical outputs at every stage, on each target, and two independent Linux runners give identical manifests. See [Reproducible builds](#reproducible-builds-owner-2026-11-07).
584:10. **Sanitizers** (owner, 2026-11-07; UBSan dropped by the owner, 2026-11-08; implemented in `scripts/checks/compiler/slice0_artifacts.sh`): every LLVM example also runs built with ASan in CI, and must give the same observation with no sanitizer report. ASan instruments only functions marked `sanitize_address`, which clang's C front end adds and the printed IR lacks, so the sanitizer build adds it to every definition (`slice0_native.sh sanitize`); a control body with an out-of-bounds load must be reported. There is no UBSan leg: UBSan's checks are inserted by clang's C front end, so on LLVM IR input it checks nothing. No C is compiled in slice 0, and the LLVM-level undefined behaviour of the printed IR (poison, `unreachable`) stays P_print's.
585:11. **Binary size** (owner, 2026-11-07; implemented, same script): the size of each example's executable (bytes and text size, pinned `llvm-size`), per backend and target, is recorded per run in `sizes.tsv`, uploaded with the job. Report-only: it never fails a run. **Compile time** (owner, 2026-11-08; same script, report-only): each example's emit step (the Lean emitter, Lean start-up included) and native step (clang and LCB's command line) in wall-clock milliseconds, next to its budget (the step's time bound), in `compile_times.tsv`.
586:12. **Stack bound** (owner, 2026-11-07; implemented in the same script and `lib/stack_bound.py`; the `aarch64-macos` run check with LLDB, 2026-11-08): a stack-bound fact for each example, derived from the final artifact (the linked executable, not the IR) for both backends, and checked against a run. The analysis follows every path of the Concrete-owned code, instruction by instruction from an explicit set of modelled instructions, (`main`, `concrete_rt_write_all`, `concrete_body`) over the disassembly's control-flow graph, tracking the stack and frame pointers' depth below `main`'s entry and every byte addressed relative to them (so the red zone), refusing an access deeper than the target's red zone below the stack pointer (128 bytes on x86-64 System V and on Apple arm64, none under AAPCS64 on Linux: deeper bytes may be overwritten by a signal handler), and fails closed on anything it does not model (an instruction outside the set, another stack-pointer write, frame- or link-register writeback, an indirect branch or call, a stack pointer copied out, an indexed stack address, a stack read of bytes the function has not itself written on every path since its last call (so no stale saved frame pointer is ever loaded), a saved slot read other than by the modelled restore, two depths meeting, recursion, a call to a function neither owned nor a PLT/stub). The external calls (`write`, `_exit`, `abort`) are listed with the depth at their entry; their own use, and that `_exit` and `abort` do not return, are the libc premise. On x86-64 Linux the run check single-steps the owned code under the pinned gdb and requires the measured depth to be within the bound, for a complete run only (stopped first at `main`, back at each external call's return address, no signal, the expected exit status). The measured depth is the lowest of the stack pointer and every stack byte a stepped instruction addresses (`lib/stack_access.py` evaluates each memory operand), so a red-zone access is counted, and one deeper than the red zone is an error. A control links a `main` that stores at the red zone's edge without moving the stack pointer and requires bound 128 and measured 128 (on AArch64 it first allocates and frees a 144-byte frame with a pre-indexed store, judged against the new stack pointer, so 144); with a 120-byte red zone the same run gives no measurement. On Mach-O each stub entry's imported symbol comes from the indirect symbol table (`llvm-objdump --macho --indirect-symbols`), since the disassembly names only the `__stubs` section. On `aarch64-macos` the run check is the same, under Apple's LLDB (`/usr/bin/lldb`, `lib/stack_measure_lldb.py`; an external call's return address is the link register); LLDB is not pinned (Apple's signed debugserver is the only one allowed to attach), so its version is printed in the gate log. `stack.tsv` records bound, measurement and externals.
